Z||000001||Fformula88||10-18-2001||10:26 PM||||I always hate to hear of sound Fiero's being junked.  I think what happens is that the owners must try trading them in, or attempt to sell them and give up.  The result is the dealer, not finding many wholesalers other than junk yards interested in 80's cars anymore let them go there.  The owners with no other way to get rid of it after a short amount of time send it to its death.  I saw an 85 last year in the yard that was full of brand new parts... some not yet installed.  Among the new items laying in the car were a complete set of wheel bearings and brake pads in the boxes.  The car also had brand new tires on all four corners and who knows what other new parts.  The owner just gave up.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfiero||10-18-2001||10:32 PM||||I agree.  I saw a 36K mile '85 SE V-6 in a California junkyard a few months ago.  There was nothing really wrong with it.....possibly a bad motor or bad clutch.  I guess the PO just didn';t want to fix it up. <P>I also saw a very nice 40K mile '84 SE in California......absolutley nothing wrong with it.  It had the cleanest interior I have ever seen on an '84 (other than trailer queens), but there it was rotting away and being picked over.<P>It really is sad.  If this coupe weren't already mostly stripped I would buy the whole thing and fix it up.  Oh well.<P>||||reg||1
